# The two things this package cannot do for you, said at the moment they
# become relevant. NVIDIA's neural renderer runtime is not redistributable,
# so no repository can ship it, and without it the program starts and the
# picture is never processed - a worse failure than not starting.

post_install() {
    cat <<'EOT'

NeuralScreen needs two things this package cannot provide:

 1. NVIDIA's neural renderer runtime, nvngx_dlssnr.dll. Put your copy at

        ~/.local/share/neuralscreen/nvngx_dlssnr.dll

    (or point NS_NR_DLL at it), then run once:

        neuralscreen --setup

    It is a Windows DLL and that is not a mistake: NVIDIA ships DLSSNR
    only as D3D12, and there is no Linux snippet for it - not in the
    driver, not on NVIDIA's OTA server. So it runs where it can, in a
    Windows process under Proton, with vkd3d-proton and the same NVIDIA
    driver underneath. --setup prepares that prefix once.

 2. A Wayland session, and an NVIDIA RTX card.

    Verified working on an RTX 3050 Laptop. NGX refuses the feature on
    anything below Blackwell - a version check, not missing kernels - so
    the program reports itself as a newer card to the check. The runtime
    carries kernels for RTX 20/30/40, which is why this works at all.
    NS_ARCH_SPOOF=0 turns it off.

    The menu's GPU dot is the honest answer: green only when the worker
    really created the feature. Grep the log for '[spoof]' and '[proton]'.

Run it with:  neuralscreen
Diagnostics:  neuralscreen --diag
              then attach ~/.local/state/neuralscreen/NeuralScreen.log
Settings:     ~/.config/neuralscreen/config.json

The first launch asks once for the screen to capture and once for the
hotkeys - that is the Wayland security model. The screen-capture answer is
remembered from then on.

Notes for your desktop:
  KDE        the overlay hides itself from screen capture, so it does not
             re-process its own output. That also hides it from OBS and
             from screenshots. Needs KWin 6.6 or newer; older KWin keeps
             working and says so in the log.
  GNOME      the overlay is an ordinary window (no layer-shell in Mutter),
             and the tray icon needs the AppIndicator extension
  wlroots    xdg-desktop-portal-wlr ships no global shortcuts; join the
             'input' group for the direct fallback

/usr/share/doc/neuralscreen/README.md has the rest.

EOT
}

post_upgrade() {
    post_install
}
